1
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:08,362
Why can FCPO prices at night be influenced by markets outside Malaysia?

2
00:00:09,102 --> 00:00:14,524
Because while Malaysia trades its night session, other major commodity markets are also open.

3
00:00:16,424 --> 00:00:22,614
In this video, we walk through the FCPO trading day, and how markets around the world can influence it.

4
00:00:25,314 --> 00:00:29,296
FCPO trades in three sessions, all in Malaysian time.

5
00:00:29,636 --> 00:00:37,890
The morning session runs from 10:30 am to 12:30 pm. The afternoon session runs from 2:30 pm to 6:00 pm.

6
00:00:38,230 --> 00:00:44,588
Then there is an after-hours session, known as T plus one, from 9:00 pm to 11:30 pm.

7
00:00:46,688 --> 00:00:51,582
The morning and afternoon sessions run on Bursa trading days, Monday to Friday.

8
00:00:51,922 --> 00:00:58,328
The after-hours session runs Monday to Thursday night. There is no T plus one session on Friday night.

9
00:00:58,668 --> 00:01:05,194
Public holidays and special schedules apply, so always check the Bursa trading calendar and your broker's notices.

10
00:01:07,294 --> 00:01:10,748
So what does T plus one mean? T stands for the trade date.

11
00:01:11,088 --> 00:01:19,846
Trades done in the after-hours session are treated as trades of the next trading day. A trade at 10 pm on Tuesday is recorded as a Wednesday trade.

12
00:01:20,186 --> 00:01:23,952
This matters for clearing, margin and your daily statements.

13
00:01:26,052 --> 00:01:32,194
Why do sessions matter? Because trading activity and volatility can differ from one session to another.

14
00:01:32,534 --> 00:01:42,492
Between sessions, during the lunch break and overnight, news can still arrive. When trading resumes, prices may open at a different level, creating a gap.

15
00:01:42,832 --> 00:01:46,574
Knowing the sessions helps you plan your orders and manage risk.

16
00:01:48,674 --> 00:01:55,248
FCPO does not trade in isolation. Several global markets are closely watched by palm oil traders.

17
00:01:55,588 --> 00:02:04,442
In China, the Dalian Commodity Exchange trades soybean oil and palm olein futures. China is a major buyer of vegetable oils.

18
00:02:04,782 --> 00:02:13,828
In the United States, the Chicago Board of Trade trades soybean oil and soybeans. Soybean oil competes with palm oil as a vegetable oil.

19
00:02:14,168 --> 00:02:25,134
Crude oil matters too, because palm oil is also used to make biodiesel. And the ringgit matters, because FCPO is priced in ringgit while palm oil is traded internationally.

20
00:02:27,234 --> 00:02:33,736
Here is how it can play out. FCPO's afternoon session closes at 6 pm, but other markets keep moving.

21
00:02:34,076 --> 00:02:41,418
News and price moves overseas can shape sentiment in the T plus one session, and when FCPO reopens the next morning.

22
00:02:41,758 --> 00:02:51,836
But these markets do not always move in the same direction. Local factors, such as production and export data, weather and the ringgit, can push FCPO the other way.

23
00:02:53,936 --> 00:02:59,886
FCPO has a morning session, an afternoon session and an after-hours T plus one session.

24
00:03:00,226 --> 00:03:03,872
After-hours trades count as the next trading day's trades.

25
00:03:04,212 --> 00:03:08,530
There is no T plus one session on Friday night, and holidays apply.

26
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:11,772
Breaks between sessions can lead to price gaps.

27
00:03:12,112 --> 00:03:16,598
Dalian, Chicago, crude oil and the ringgit can influence sentiment.

28
00:03:16,938 --> 00:03:20,608
But related markets do not always move in the same direction.

29
00:03:21,908 --> 00:03:30,762
Commodity markets are not only influenced by what happens in Malaysia. Understanding trading hours and global market links helps us see the bigger picture.

30
00:03:32,862 --> 00:03:36,628
This is only the foundation for understanding the futures market.

31
00:03:36,968 --> 00:03:39,966
Know the product you trade before taking any risk.

32
